WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.720 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A playground designer turns a square tile and notices that
it sometimes looks exactly as it did before.

00:00:01.720 --> 00:00:09.360 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A long rectangular tile behaves differently.

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:14.560 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Why do some shapes match their original outline several
times during one full turn?

00:00:14.560 --> 00:00:19.362 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Let us investigate turns that move a shape and turns that
reveal its symmetry.

00:00:19.362 --> 00:00:19.842 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Today we are learning to rotate shapes about a pivot and
investigate rotational symmetry.

00:00:19.842 --> 00:00:30.665 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
We will build an explanation using models, rather than
memorising a rule without understanding it.

00:00:30.665 --> 00:00:36.025 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Our learning goal is to rotate shapes about a pivot and
investigate rotational symmetry.

00:00:36.025 --> 00:00:41.660 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
As you investigate, connect what you can see, what you can
make, and the mathematical language you use.

00:00:41.660 --> 00:00:44.420 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
First, I can describe a turn precisely.

00:00:44.420 --> 00:00:47.740 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Second, I can check a rotation using its pivot.

00:00:47.740 --> 00:00:51.340 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Third, I can find an order of rotational symmetry.

00:00:51.340 --> 00:00:54.806 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Keep these goals in mind as you explain each discovery.

00:00:54.806 --> 00:00:55.366 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Think about a door turning on a hinge or a clock hand moving
around its centre.

00:00:55.366 --> 00:01:02.206 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A rotation needs a fixed pivot.

00:01:02.206 --> 00:01:03.606 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Every point turns through the same angle about that pivot.

00:01:03.606 --> 00:01:11.968 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The shape changes its direction and often its position,
while the pivot remains fixed throughout the movement.

00:01:11.968 --> 00:01:12.328 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
To give a complete rotation instruction, state the pivot,
angle and direction.

00:01:12.328 --> 00:01:16.688 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A quarter turn means ninety degrees.

00:01:16.688 --> 00:01:22.848 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A half turn means one hundred and eighty degrees.

00:01:22.848 --> 00:01:28.088 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Clockwise follows the direction of clock hands, while
anticlockwise goes the other way.

00:01:28.088 --> 00:01:32.569 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Leaving out one of these details can make an instruction
ambiguous.

00:01:32.569 --> 00:01:34.449 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Our pivot is at four, four.

00:01:34.449 --> 00:01:36.969 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The outlined shape sits to its right.

00:01:36.969 --> 00:01:41.009 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Choose a marked point on the shape and notice its distance
from the pivot.

00:01:41.009 --> 00:01:48.190 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
During the turn that point follows a circular path, keeping
the same distance from the pivot instead of sliding along a
straight route.

00:02:00.990 --> 00:02:01.470 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Turn the shape ninety degrees anticlockwise around four,
four.

00:02:01.470 --> 00:02:01.630 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The part that began to the right of the pivot swings
upwards.

00:02:01.630 --> 00:02:02.230 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Keep the original outline as a reference.

00:02:02.230 --> 00:02:06.226 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Every vertex makes the same quarter turn, so the connecting
sides and corner angles preserve the original shape’s
geometry.

00:02:06.226 --> 00:02:09.866 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Imagine a point at six, four, two units right of the pivot.

00:02:09.866 --> 00:02:14.866 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
After the quarter turn anticlockwise, it is two units above
the pivot, at four, six.

00:02:14.866 --> 00:02:18.626 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
This check uses the pivot’s position as well as the turn.

00:02:18.626 --> 00:02:23.427 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Rotating around the origin would be a different instruction
and give a different result.

00:02:23.427 --> 00:02:27.267 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Return to the original shape and keep the pivot at four,
four.

00:02:27.267 --> 00:02:32.027 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Where would the point at six, four finish after a
ninety-degree clockwise turn?

00:02:32.027 --> 00:02:36.827 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Think of moving from the right-hand direction towards the
downward direction.

00:02:36.827 --> 00:02:41.043 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Predict the full coordinates and justify the distance from
the pivot.

00:02:46.043 --> 00:02:48.043 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The point finishes at four, two.

00:02:48.043 --> 00:02:51.803 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
It is still two units from the pivot, but now directly below
it.

00:02:51.803 --> 00:02:55.323 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Compare this with the anticlockwise result four, six.

00:02:55.323 --> 00:03:01.586 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Both turns have the same angle, yet their different
directions place the point on opposite sides of the pivot.

00:03:01.586 --> 00:03:06.146 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A learner moves every vertex three squares right and calls
it a quarter turn.

00:03:06.146 --> 00:03:06.626 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
That is a translation, not a rotation.

00:03:06.626 --> 00:03:14.586 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A rotation keeps the pivot fixed and changes the directions
from it to the vertices.

00:03:14.586 --> 00:03:19.991 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Use the point’s circular path and the changed orientation to
explain the difference positively.

00:03:19.991 --> 00:03:22.151 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Return to the original shape and make a half turn about the
same pivot.

00:03:22.151 --> 00:03:27.951 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A point two units right of the pivot moves two units left of
it.

00:03:27.951 --> 00:03:35.901 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
For a half turn, clockwise and anticlockwise reach the same
final position, although the paths travel around different
sides along the way.

00:03:35.901 --> 00:03:41.101 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Any shape can be rotated, but not every turn makes it match
its original outline.

00:03:41.101 --> 00:03:47.661 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Rotational symmetry asks about the special turns where the
whole shape fits exactly onto its starting position.

00:03:47.661 --> 00:03:54.371 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Compare every side and corner, rather than accepting a rough
resemblance or matching only one particular point.

00:03:54.371 --> 00:03:58.371 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Place the pivot at the square’s centre and rotate through
one full turn.

00:03:58.371 --> 00:04:04.411 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
It matches at ninety, one hundred and eighty, two hundred
and seventy, and three hundred and sixty degrees.

00:04:04.411 --> 00:04:06.531 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
These four matching positions give the square rotational
symmetry of order four.

00:04:06.531 --> 00:04:12.892 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The starting position is not counted a second time.

00:04:12.892 --> 00:04:16.411 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Now try a non-square rectangle about its centre.

00:04:16.411 --> 00:04:20.691 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Will a quarter turn match, or do its unequal side lengths
prevent that?

00:04:20.691 --> 00:04:26.971 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Predict how many matching positions occur in one full turn,
including the final full-turn match.

00:04:26.971 --> 00:04:30.953 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Explain what happens to the long and short sides as it
turns.

00:04:35.953 --> 00:04:38.473 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A non-square rectangle matches after a half turn and again
after a full turn.

00:04:38.473 --> 00:04:47.113 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A quarter turn exchanges its long-side and short-side
directions, so its outline does not fit.

00:04:47.113 --> 00:04:49.713 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
It has order two, rather than order four.

00:04:49.713 --> 00:04:55.364 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The requirement is an exact whole-outline match, not simply
another rectangular picture.

00:04:55.364 --> 00:05:00.204 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
An equilateral triangle has three equal sides and three
equal angles.

00:05:00.204 --> 00:05:07.444 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
About its centre, it matches after turns of one hundred and
twenty, two hundred and forty, and three hundred and sixty
degrees.

00:05:07.444 --> 00:05:09.004 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
That gives order three.

00:05:09.004 --> 00:05:13.462 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The spacing between matching positions is equal throughout
the full turn.

00:05:13.462 --> 00:05:16.782 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A scalene triangle has unequal side lengths.

00:05:16.782 --> 00:05:25.382 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
It does not match its starting outline under a smaller
positive turn about its centre, but it does match after a
complete three-hundred-and-sixty-degree turn.

00:05:25.382 --> 00:05:31.883 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
We describe this as order one: there is no non-trivial
rotational symmetry to discover before the full turn.

00:05:31.883 --> 00:05:36.843 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Lines of symmetry and rotational symmetry describe different
transformations.

00:05:36.843 --> 00:05:44.523 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A non-square rectangle has mirror lines through the
midpoints of opposite sides, and it also has order-two
rotational symmetry.

00:05:44.523 --> 00:05:49.643 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Do not assume that seeing a mirror line automatically tells
you every matching turn.

00:05:49.643 --> 00:05:53.112 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Test the particular transformation you are asked about.

00:05:53.112 --> 00:05:57.472 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Choose a paper shape, mark a pivot and trace its original
outline.

00:05:57.472 --> 00:06:03.872 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Turn it carefully through one full revolution, recording
every exact match after the starting position.

00:06:03.872 --> 00:06:08.552 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
State your evidence with an angle or fraction-of-a-turn
description in words.

00:06:08.552 --> 00:06:11.610 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Check that the pivot did not drift while you were turning.

00:06:11.610 --> 00:06:17.170 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A shape’s rotational-symmetry investigation normally uses
its appropriate centre.

00:06:17.170 --> 00:06:23.930 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A different pivot can move the shape away from the starting
outline, even when the angle is a familiar symmetry turn.

00:06:23.930 --> 00:06:30.831 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
This is another reason that a complete rotation instruction
must name the pivot as well as angle and direction.

00:06:30.831 --> 00:06:37.311 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
You can now describe rotations, check a point’s distance
from a pivot and investigate exact matching turns.

00:06:37.311 --> 00:06:44.671 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The square, non-square rectangle and equilateral triangle
have different rotational orders for mathematical reasons.

00:06:44.671 --> 00:06:50.725 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Choose a new tile shape and predict its order, then test the
whole outline before accepting your prediction.
